Directed vs. undirected. A directed edge means A connects to B but not necessarily the reverse; an undirected edge treats every tie as mutual. In this lab, the SBM network is undirected, so all algorithms treat connections as reciprocal. If the network were directed, centrality rankings would change: a node that receives many incoming ties but sends few would have high in-degree but low out-degree, a distinction that collapses into a single score when edges are undirected. Community detection would also shift, since asymmetric ties between groups could reveal directional flows that symmetrizing the network would hide.\
Weighted vs. unweighted. Unweighted degree counts connections; weighted degree sums edge weights. In the lab's unweighted SBM, a node with three weak ties ranks the same as one with three strong ties. Adding weights could reshuffle centrality rankings by letting fewer but heavier connections outrank many thin ones. Community detection would shift too, since modularity optimization would favor clusters linked by strong ties over clusters linked by many weak ones.\
