\cf0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The three centrality measures produce largely non-overlapping top-10 lists. Degree and betweenness share zero nodes in common, and degree and eigenvector also share zero. Betweenness and eigenvector overlap on 5 nodes (352, 129, 394, 291, 390), but no single node appears in all three lists. This divergence reflects the fact that each measure captures a different dimension of importance. Degree rewards raw connectivity -- the top degree nodes (163, 378, 158) simply have the most edges, and their indices suggest they sit in the largest block (community 0, nodes 0-399). Betweenness surfaces nodes like 530 and 570 that sit at community boundaries and broker shortest paths between blocks, even if they lack high degree. Eigenvector centrality rewards nodes embedded in dense, well-connected neighborhoods, which is why it partially overlaps with betweenness (both are sensitive to position within the broader network structure) but not with degree (which only sees local edge count). The results confirm that "importance" is not a single concept -- the choice of centrality measure encodes an assumption about what kind of influence matters.
